70 questions, judged twice: on the answer, and on the route.

First specification: the tool must match where the gold answer came from.

                                  right route  wrong route
  right answer                              9           60
  wrong answer                              1            0

  outcome evaluation alone passes  69 of 70
  adding that trajectory rule      9 of 70

Before blaming the system for those 60, read three of them:

  What was revenue in 2023 Q1?
    spec wanted search_documents, it used query_warehouse
  How many orders were there in 2023 Q1?
    spec wanted search_documents, it used query_warehouse
  What was gross margin in 2023 Q1?
    spec wanted search_documents, it used query_warehouse

The specification is wrong. Those cases are labelled `document` because their
gold answer was verified against a document span — that is a statement about
provenance, not about which tool ought to answer. Meridian's revenue is in the
warehouse *and* in the review, and going to the warehouse for a figure is the
better route, not a deviation.

This is the failure mode of trajectory evaluation in one paragraph. A
trajectory spec is a claim about how the system should work, written by
somebody who may be wrong, and when it disagrees with the system the burden of
proof is genuinely on both.

Second specification, and the one worth keeping: every answer must come from
some tool. Either source is fine; no source is not.

  answers with at least one source call   70 of 70
  answers produced from memory alone      0 of 70

That rule is narrow enough to be right and cheap enough to run on every case.
It also generalises, which the first one did not: it is a property of the run
rather than a prediction about it.

Where trajectory evaluation genuinely earns its keep is agents that write,
spend, or send. 'Got the right answer' is not a sufficient account of a run
that also despatched two emails on the way, and no outcome check will ever
mention them.
